---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import HeaderAnalyzer from '../../components/HeaderAnalyzer.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="HTTP Header Analyzer" description="Paste HTTP response headers and get a plain-English explanation of what each one does.">
  <HeaderAnalyzer client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why headers carry more meaning than they look like</h2>
  <p>A response's headers often explain behavior that's otherwise invisible — why a browser cached (or didn't cache) a response, why a CORS request failed, or what security policies are active — all before you even look at the response body.</p>

  <h2>How to use this analyzer</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Paste raw response headers (from browser dev tools or a curl -I command).</li>
    <li>See a plain-English explanation of each recognized header.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Ignoring security headers</strong> (or their absence) when reviewing a response — missing headers like CSP or X-Frame-Options can indicate a security gap.</li>
    <li><strong>Not checking caching headers</strong> when debugging why a browser is showing stale content.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Use cases</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Understanding an unfamiliar header seen in browser dev tools</li>
    <li>Debugging caching or CORS issues by reviewing the actual response headers</li>
    <li>Reviewing a third-party API's response headers for security posture</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Does this work with request headers too, or only responses?</strong> It's designed primarily for response headers, though many header meanings apply similarly if pasted from a request.</p>
</ToolLayout>
